## Migration Analysis

Your code has three patterns that break on .NET 8:

| API | Problem on .NET 8 | Pattern |
|-----|-------------------|---------|
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9), unreliable | Blocking call interruption |
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` | Cancellable work loop fallback |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Works, but not cancellation-aware | Blocking call interruption |

There's also a **race condition** in the original: if multiple items are enqueued before `WaitOne` returns, `Reset()` clears the signal and subsequent items may stall.

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _runTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _runTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Normal shutdown — nothing to do.
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);
    }

    public async Task StopAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();          // signal no more items
        _cts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // timeout fallback
        if (_runTask is not null)
            await _runTask;
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

## What Changed and Why

| Before | After | Reason |
|--------|-------|--------|
| `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` + `lock` | `Channel<string>` | Thread-safe producer/consumer with built-in signaling; eliminates the race condition |
| `Thread` + `while(true)` | `Task.Run` + `await foreach` | Cooperative cancellation; no blocked thread while idle |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `Channel.Writer.Complete()` | Gracefully signals the loop to drain and exit |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter(5s)` | Cooperative timeout — `ReadAllAsync` checks the token |
| `ThreadAbortException` (implicit) | `OperationCanceledException` | The standard cancellation exception in modern .NET |

## Key Pitfalls to Watch

1. **`CancellationToken` doesn't interrupt `Handle(item)`** — if `Handle` is long-running, pass the token into it and check `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at safe points.
2. **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** — it's `IDisposable`; leaking it causes timer/callback leaks.
3. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** — the `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` guard ensures only intentional cancellations are caught.
4. **Callers must `await StopAsync()`** — if your caller is synchronous, use `StopAsync().GetAwaiter().GetResult()` as a last resort, but prefer async all the way up.

If you need a **synchronous-only** version (no async/await), replace `Channel` with `BlockingCollection<string>` and use `GetConsumingEnumerable(token)`:

```csharp
// Synchronous alternative
private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();

private void RunLoop(CancellationToken token)
{
    try
    {
        foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(token))
            Handle(item);
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested) { }
}

public void Stop()
{
    _queue.CompleteAdding();
    _cts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
    _runTask?.Wait();
}
```
